In order to ensure consistent Git commits and to enable clear identification with what each commit message does, following these standards is key to achieving this. 

- To commit, start the git message with [git commit]

Next should be the type of commit and the changes made:

doc -  Documentation changes or updates
feat - A new feature has been added to the codebase
fix - A bug fix or correction to resolve an issue
style - Changes related to code formatting, indentation or whitespace
refactor - Code refactor without adding new features/fixing bugs
test - Addition or modification of test cases.
chore - Other changes not directly affecting the code (build scripts, dependencies)

After identifying the type of commit, next comes the message, describing the type of commit.

- The message should be descriptive, no more than 100 characters long, describing in detail the changes made.

"This commit ..."

The structure is as follows

git commit -am "[type]: [message]"

